one thing i love about rust's macros is that you can accomplish this without needing a separate file type or inventing syntax
#[component]
pub fn HomePage(user: Option<User>) -> Element {
rsx! {
Layout {
title: "Home",
if let Some(u) = user {
h1 { "Welcome back, {u.name}!" }
a { href: "/users/{u.id}", "Your profile" }
} else {
h1 { "Welcome, guest!" }
a { href: "/login", "Log in" }
}
}
}
}

It's quite astonishing how the historical inability of commonly used programming languages (from FORTRAN all the way to, say, Java/C#) to ergonomically return
two
values from a function call has caused people to invent all kinds of insane APIs/contracts.
[double x, errno_t errno] = strtod(s, nullptr);
if (errno != 0) {
// ...
}
[x, _] = strtod(s, nullptr);  // When you're certain the error is impossible or just don't care
Instead, we're stuck with "check if the return value is -1 and if it is, check the global variable errno" at best, and with "the function doesn't really reliably reports its errors so don't feed it malformed data, idc" at worst.

